e epic itself logs), it may exceed a sprint and need\nre-splitting.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nNo Investigation epics — correctly justified: the strategy explicitly\ndeclares no open questions, and no declared risk changes which epics exist.\nThe convention epic (E001) is correctly typed Implementation: it produces a\npublished artifact (schema + guidance), and the summary explicitly reasons\nthrough why it is not an Investigation. The serving-runtimes upstream risk is\ncorrectly handled as an assumption + AC on E004 rather than a gate — its\noutcome would change effort, not the epic set. All epics have `gated_by:\nnull` and no Investigation dependencies exist, so no gating metadata is\nrequired; nothing inconsistent.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nAll ten epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (not\n`investigation_signals`); all ten ai-signals rationale files exist and their\nvalues match frontmatter. Spot-checks hold up well: E001's conservative\nscores (existing_foundation 0 for greenfield convention, open_questions 0\nbecause the design decisions are the work itself, repo_access 0 with no\npublication location named, human_process_gates -1 for cross-team sign-off)\nare each justified; E004's external_dependency 0 and architecture_claims 0\nare grounded in the real upstream/context-file gaps; E010's\nhuman_process_gates -1 reflects the docs review process. Per instructions,\ncomputed `ai_implementability` values were not re-derived.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the HLRs. Rule-mandated ACs are\npresent on every epic that meets the rule's criteria: all nine replacement\nepics (E001–E009 where they replace log output — E002 through E009) carry the\nrevertibility/rollback AC, and the docs-authoring epic E010 carries the\ntechnical-review AC. No konflux chain exists, so the Rule 25 build-pipeline\nAC correctly does not apply. No epic body references sibling epic